% The checking walk: declarations, then rules, then function equations —
% or, for a query, declarations and then goals.
%
% This module decides *what* to ask the solver: it walks the encoded
% AST and tells `check_*` constraints into `solver.chr`. The
% declarations come first (`tell_decls`), then each rule, then each
% function's equations, and a class function's equations are tried
% against its signatures one attempt at a time.
%
% Why rules and not functions: a function can neither tell a constraint
% nor make a fresh logical variable, and the walk does both at nearly
% every node. Everything that needs neither — the environment lookups,
% the source-type encoding, the assoc-list bookkeeping — stays a pure
% function, in this file or in `env.chr` / `skel.chr`.
%
% `check_attempt` is exported not because another module calls it but
% because `run_chr_session` resolves a goal's constraint name through
% the export map: the class-function overload search below runs each
% (equation x signature) attempt in a throwaway sub-session.
%
% The list traversals here go through `library(lists)` rather than
% hand-rolled recursion; the cost of that is recorded once, in
% `skel.chr`'s header.
:- module('$tc_walk', [
    walk_program/3,
    walk_goals/4,
    tell_decls/3,
    check_attempt/8
]).

:- use_module('$tc_ast').
:- use_module('$tc_diag').
:- use_module('$tc_env').
:- use_module('$tc_skel').
:- use_module('$typechecker').
:- use_module(library(lists)).
:- use_module(library(maybe)).
:- use_module(library(meta)).
:- use_module(library(pairs)).

% Everything a use site needs to know about the scope it sits in.
% Mirrors `CheckCtx`: the unit's variable slots, the ambient signatures
% its enclosing bounded declarations contribute, and the diagnostic
% context its emissions are attributed to. The program-wide environment
% rides along so that a walker constraint takes one argument instead of
% two.
:- chr_type scope ---> scope(tc_env,
                             list(pair(any, ty)),
                             list(pair(amb_key, list(fun_sig_t))),
                             ctx).

% Which kind of position a constructor application's fields are
% recursed into as (docs/reference/type-system.md §Expression Typing:
% an evaluated position is typed by what it evaluates to, a structural
% one by the term it denotes). A CHR constraint cannot take the typing
% function as a parameter, so the choice travels as a tag and each
% arm dispatches on it.
%
% Not to be confused with a *mode* in the instantiation sense, which the
% checker does not track at all (§No mode checking).
:- chr_type arg_position ---> evaluated_position ; structural_position.

% What a `guard_match` leaves for the `guard_getarg`s that read the same
% term: the canonicalized constructor name, and one slot per field at
% the instantiation the match determined. Reading a field type out of a
% slot rather than re-deriving it from the declaration is what makes
% extraction work at a rigid scrutinee. The slot list is empty when the
% match was not emitted at all (an undeclared constructor, or one used
% at the wrong arity), which sends the following extractions down the
% declaration path. Mirrors `MatchState`.
:- chr_type match_state ---> match_state(any, list(ty)).

:- function scope_env(scope) -> tc_env.
scope_env(scope(Env, _, _, _)) -> Env.

:- function scope_cons(scope) -> con_env.
scope_cons(S) -> env_cons(scope_env(S)).

:- function scope_ctx(scope) -> ctx.
scope_ctx(scope(_, _, _, C)) -> C.

% A source variable's pre-allocated type slot. `collect_vars_rule` walks
% the same nodes this walk visits, so every variable has one; a miss
% would be a collector bug, and typing the variable `any` is the
% disposition that reports the least about it. Nothing in this language
% can raise a diagnostic error from source, so a collector omission
% shows up as a silently *missing* diagnostic rather than as a crash:
% add a golden whenever the collector learns a new node.
%
% The lookup is linear, so a unit's checking cost is quadratic in its
% distinct variables; see the note in `skel.chr`'s header.
:- function scope_var_ty(scope, any) -> ty.
scope_var_ty(scope(_, Vars, _, _), V) -> slot_or_any(assoc_get(V, Vars)).

:- function slot_or_any(maybe(ty)) -> ty.
slot_or_any(just(T)) -> T.
slot_or_any(nothing) -> ty_any.

% A shadowing binding goes in front: `assoc_get` stops at the first
% match, so prepending is a left-biased union. Two callers
% shadow — a lambda's parameters, which must neither inherit an
% enclosing variable's type nor leak their own back out, and a
% function-body `is`, which rebinds its variable to a fresh slot.
:- function scope_shadow(scope, list(pair(any, ty))) -> scope.
scope_shadow(scope(Env, Vars, Amb, C), New) -> scope(Env, append(New, Vars), Amb, C).

% The ambient signatures visible for one `(name, arity)` in this scope.
:- function scope_ambient(scope, amb_key) -> list(fun_sig_t).
scope_ambient(scope(_, _, Amb, _), Key) -> from_maybe([], assoc_get(Key, Amb)).

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Source types to solver types
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% A `tvar` resolves through the caller's map, which decides whether the
% declaration's type parameters are flexible (a use site, where
% `copy_term` freshens them again anyway) or rigid (an implementation
% site). The base types are recognized by their bare names applied to
% no arguments: a qualified `m:int` mangles to `m__int` and correctly is
% not the built-in, and `int(X)` is an ordinary type reference.

:- function encode_ty(list(pair(string, ty)), ty_expr) -> ty.
encode_ty(TVars, tvar(V)) -> from_maybe(ty_any, assoc_get(V, TVars)).
encode_ty(_, tconx(N, [])) | N == quote(int) -> ty_int.
encode_ty(_, tconx(N, [])) | N == quote(float) -> ty_float.
encode_ty(_, tconx(N, [])) | N == quote(string) -> ty_string.
encode_ty(_, tconx(N, [])) | N == quote(any) -> ty_any.
encode_ty(TVars, tconx(N, Args)) -> tcon(N, encode_tys(TVars, Args)).
encode_ty(TVars, tfunx(Args, Ret)) ->
    fun(encode_tys(TVars, Args), encode_ty(TVars, Ret)).

:- function encode_tys(list(pair(string, ty)), list(ty_expr)) -> list(ty).
encode_tys(TVars, Ts) -> maplist(fun(T) -> encode_ty(TVars, T) end, Ts).

% One `nbound` per bound of a declaration, against the declaration's
% shared type-variable map — which is what lets a single `copy_term` at
% the use site freshen the signature and its bounds consistently.
:- function encode_bounds(list(pair(string, ty)), list(bound_sig)) -> list(bound_named).
encode_bounds(TVars, Bs) -> maplist(fun(B) -> encode_bound(TVars, B) end, Bs).

:- function encode_bound(list(pair(string, ty)), bound_sig) -> bound_named.
encode_bound(TVars, bound_sig(N, _, Args, Ret, _)) ->
    nbound(N, encode_tys(TVars, Args), encode_ty(TVars, Ret)).

% --- Type variables of a declaration --------------------------------

:- function collect_ty_vars(list(ty_expr)) -> list(string).
collect_ty_vars(Ts) -> concat_map(fun collect_ty_vars_expr/1, Ts).

:- function collect_ty_vars_expr(ty_expr) -> list(string).
collect_ty_vars_expr(tvar(V)) -> [V].
collect_ty_vars_expr(tconx(_, Args)) -> collect_ty_vars(Args).
collect_ty_vars_expr(tfunx(Args, Ret)) ->
    append(collect_ty_vars(Args), collect_ty_vars_expr(Ret)).

:- function bound_sigs_vars(list(bound_sig)) -> list(string).
bound_sigs_vars(Bs) -> concat_map(fun bound_sig_vars/1, Bs).

:- function bound_sig_vars(bound_sig) -> list(string).
bound_sig_vars(bound_sig(_, _, Args, Ret, _)) ->
    append(collect_ty_vars(Args), collect_ty_vars_expr(Ret)).

% The distinct type-variable names of a declaration, ascending.
%
% The order is load-bearing, not cosmetic: a rigid type variable's
% synthetic id is user-visible (`T#0` in an inconsistency message), and
% one id is allocated per distinct name in this order — ascending, not
% order of appearance (`test/golden/typecheck_tvar_order/`). Flexible
% allocation does not care, but it goes through the same sort so there
% is one rule to remember.
:- function sort_unique_tvars(list(string)) -> list(string).
sort_unique_tvars(Vs) -> sort_by(fun(A, B) -> A < B end, distinct(Vs)).

% The declaration's parameters in *declaration* order, as the parent
% type of a constructor signature spells them.
:- function tvar_slots(list(string), list(pair(string, ty))) -> list(ty).
tvar_slots(Vs, TVars) ->
    maplist(fun(V) -> from_maybe(ty_any, assoc_get(V, TVars)) end, Vs).

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Allocation
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------

:- chr_constraint
    alloc_flex_tvars(list(string), list(pair(string, ty))),
    alloc_rigid_tvars(list(string), list(pair(string, ty))),
    decl_rigid_supply(int),
    make_decl_rigid(ty),
    make_decl_rigids(int, list(ty)),
    fresh_slots(int, list(ty)),
    encode_one_sig(fun_sig, fun_sig_t),
    encode_sigs(list(fun_sig), list(fun_sig_t)).

% A flexible slot is just an unbound variable, so there is nothing to
% build: the pair's value stays as it was created.
alloc_flex_tvars_nil @
    alloc_flex_tvars([], Out) <=> Out = [].

alloc_flex_tvars_cons @
    alloc_flex_tvars([V|Vs], Out) <=>
    alloc_flex_tvars(Vs, Rest),
    Out = [kv(V, _Slot) | Rest].

% Rigids are allocated head-first so the ids ascend with the (sorted)
% name list.
alloc_rigid_tvars_nil @
    alloc_rigid_tvars([], Out) <=> Out = [].

alloc_rigid_tvars_cons @
    alloc_rigid_tvars([V|Vs], Out) <=>
    make_decl_rigid(T),
    alloc_rigid_tvars(Vs, Rest),
    Out = [kv(V, T) | Rest].

% The declaration-site rigid supply. It counts *up* from 0 while the
% solver's own `rigid_supply` counts down from -1, so the two ranges
% cannot collide — and this is the range a reader actually sees, since
% every polymorphic declaration's own parameters are allocated from it.
%
% The pin cell is a body-only variable, i.e. a fresh unbound logical
% variable, so guard-derived evidence can pin it later.
make_decl_rigid1 @
    decl_rigid_supply(N), make_decl_rigid(Out) <=>
    N1 is N + 1,
    Out = rigid(_Cell, N),
    decl_rigid_supply(N1).

make_decl_rigids_done @
    make_decl_rigids(N, Out) <=> N =< 0 | Out = [].

make_decl_rigids_step @
    make_decl_rigids(N, Out) <=>
    make_decl_rigid(B),
    make_decl_rigids(N - 1, Rest),
    Out = [B | Rest].

fresh_slots_done @
    fresh_slots(N, Out) <=> N =< 0 | Out = [].

fresh_slots_step @
    fresh_slots(N, Out) <=>
    fresh_slots(N - 1, Rest),
    Out = [_Slot | Rest].

% One declared signature instantiated at fresh flexible variables, with
% the variables shared between the arguments and the return type.
encode_one_sig1 @
    encode_one_sig(fun_sig(Args, Ret), Out) <=>
    alloc_flex_tvars(sort_unique_tvars(append(collect_ty_vars(Args),
                                              collect_ty_vars_expr(Ret))),
                     TVars),
    Out is fsig(encode_tys(TVars, Args), encode_ty(TVars, Ret)).

encode_sigs_nil @
    encode_sigs([], Out) <=> Out = [].

encode_sigs_cons @
    encode_sigs([S|Ss], Out) <=>
    encode_one_sig(S, Sig),
    encode_sigs(Ss, Rest),
    Out = [Sig | Rest].

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Declaration facts
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% Each declaration is instantiated once, at fresh *flexible*
% variables; use sites `copy_term` the stored fact, so nothing a use
% site does can pollute it.
%
% The facts are built as *data* first and told from the list, rather
% than told directly at each declaration. That is what lets the
% class-function overload search hand a whole declaration environment
% to a throwaway sub-session (`retell_decls` below) instead of
% re-deriving it from the AST every attempt — which was 38% of all the
% time the checker spent on the golden corpus.

% One declaration fact, as a term. One constructor per solver
% constraint the declarations produce, with the same argument types.
:- chr_type decl_fact ---> df_constraint_sig(any, int, list(ty))
                         ; df_constraint_bounds(any, int, list(bound_named))
                         ; df_function_sig(any, int, fun_sig_t)
                         ; df_function_sigs(any, int, list(fun_sig_t))
                         ; df_function_bounds(any, int, list(bound_named))
                         ; df_con_sig(any, con_sig_t).

:- chr_constraint
    tell_decls(tc_env, program, list(decl_fact)),
    retell_decls(list(decl_fact)),
    tell_facts(list(decl_fact)),
    tell_fact(decl_fact),
    build_constraint_facts(list(pair(con_key, list(ty_expr))),
                           list(pair(con_key, list(bound_sig))),
                           list(decl_fact)),
    constraint_bound_facts(any, int, list(bound_sig), list(pair(string, ty)),
                           list(decl_fact)),
    build_function_facts(list(fun_def), list(decl_fact)),
    build_con_facts(list(type_def), list(decl_fact)),
    build_td_con_facts(type_def, list(data_con), list(decl_fact)).

tell_decls1 @
    tell_decls(_, program(_, Funs, ConTypes, ConBounds, Tds), Out) <=>
    build_constraint_facts(ConTypes, ConBounds, ConFacts),
    build_function_facts(Funs, FunFacts),
    build_con_facts(Tds, CtorFacts),
    Facts is append(ConFacts, append(FunFacts, CtorFacts)),
    tell_facts(Facts),
    Out = Facts.

% The same declaration environment, in a session that does not have it
% yet. Everything is copied first, so no *stored* fact shares a
% variable with the session the list came from: an attempt runs inside
% `run_chr_session`, where a shared logical variable is *the same
% variable*, and a fact bound in the sub-session would corrupt the
% outer one silently. Copying makes that impossible rather than merely
% unlikely — and it is still far cheaper than re-deriving the facts,
% which is O(size of every declaration in the program). (The
% uncopied list does reach the sub-session, as this constraint's own
% argument, so the two short-lived suspensions that carry it register
% as observers of the outer variables. They are killed immediately and
% each session drops foreign observer ids, so nothing comes of it.)
%
% Only the *sharing* half of this is tested. Deleting the `copy_term`
% leaves the whole suite green, because no solver rule today unifies
% against a stored declaration fact without freshening it first; if one
% is ever added, the cross-session corruption is silent. Keep the copy.
%
% The whole list is copied in ONE call, and that is load-bearing: a
% bounded declaration contributes two facts — the signature and the
% bounds — whose type variables are shared, and the use site pairs them
% in a single rule head (`bounded_function_match`). Freshen them
% independently and the bound lands at variables the call never binds,
% so it stays residual and succeeds silently.
% `test/golden/typecheck_class_bounded_call/` pins that.
retell_decls1 @
    retell_decls(Facts) <=>
    Fresh is copy_term(quote(Facts)),
    tell_facts(Fresh).

tell_facts_nil @
    tell_facts([]) <=> true.

tell_facts_cons @
    tell_facts([F|Fs]) <=> tell_fact(F), tell_facts(Fs).

tell_fact_constraint_sig @
    tell_fact(df_constraint_sig(N, A, Tys)) <=> constraint_sig(N, A, Tys).

tell_fact_constraint_bounds @
    tell_fact(df_constraint_bounds(N, A, Bs)) <=> constraint_bounds(N, A, Bs).

tell_fact_function_sig @
    tell_fact(df_function_sig(N, A, Sig)) <=> function_sig(N, A, Sig).

tell_fact_function_sigs @
    tell_fact(df_function_sigs(N, A, Sigs)) <=> function_sigs(N, A, Sigs).

tell_fact_function_bounds @
    tell_fact(df_function_bounds(N, A, Bs)) <=> function_bounds(N, A, Bs).

tell_fact_con_sig @
    tell_fact(df_con_sig(N, Sig)) <=> con_sig(N, Sig).

build_constraint_facts_nil @
    build_constraint_facts([], _, Out) <=> Out = [].

% The argument types and the bounds share one type-variable map, so a
% single `copy_term` at the use site freshens both consistently.
build_constraint_facts_cons @
    build_constraint_facts([kv(con_key(N, A), ArgTys)|Rest], Bounds, Out) <=>
    Bs is from_maybe([], assoc_get(con_key(N, A), Bounds)),
    alloc_flex_tvars(sort_unique_tvars(append(collect_ty_vars(ArgTys),
                                              bound_sigs_vars(Bs))),
                     TVars),
    constraint_bound_facts(N, A, Bs, TVars, BoundFacts),
    build_constraint_facts(Rest, Bounds, More),
    Tys is encode_tys(TVars, ArgTys),
    Tail is append(BoundFacts, More),
    Out = [df_constraint_sig(N, A, Tys) | Tail].

constraint_bound_facts_none @
    constraint_bound_facts(_, _, [], _, Out) <=> Out = [].

constraint_bound_facts_some @
    constraint_bound_facts(N, A, Bs, TVars, Out) <=>
    Encoded is encode_bounds(TVars, Bs),
    Out = [df_constraint_bounds(N, A, Encoded)].

build_function_facts_nil @
    build_function_facts([], Out) <=> Out = [].

% No annotations: the all-`any` signature, which mentions no type
% variables. A bounded function always has one signature (the resolver
% guarantees it), so a missing one implies no bounds.
build_function_facts_untyped @
    build_function_facts([fun_def(N, A, [], _, _)|Fs], Out) <=>
    build_function_facts(Fs, More),
    Slots is replicate(A, ty_any),
    Out = [df_function_sig(N, A, fsig(Slots, ty_any)) | More].

build_function_facts_bounded @
    build_function_facts([fun_def(N, A, [fun_sig(Args, Ret)], [B|Bs], _)|Fs], Out) <=>
    alloc_flex_tvars(sort_unique_tvars(append(append(collect_ty_vars(Args),
                                                     collect_ty_vars_expr(Ret)),
                                              bound_sigs_vars([B|Bs]))),
                     TVars),
    build_function_facts(Fs, More),
    ArgTys is encode_tys(TVars, Args),
    RetTy is encode_ty(TVars, Ret),
    Encoded is encode_bounds(TVars, [B|Bs]),
    Out = [df_function_sig(N, A, fsig(ArgTys, RetTy)),
           df_function_bounds(N, A, Encoded)
           | More].

build_function_facts_single @
    build_function_facts([fun_def(N, A, [S], _, _)|Fs], Out) <=>
    encode_one_sig(S, Sig),
    build_function_facts(Fs, More),
    Out = [df_function_sig(N, A, Sig) | More].

% More than one declared signature is a class function: the candidates
% go to the residual overload resolution, each instantiated separately.
build_function_facts_multi @
    build_function_facts([fun_def(N, A, Sigs, _, _)|Fs], Out) <=>
    encode_sigs(Sigs, Encoded),
    build_function_facts(Fs, More),
    Out = [df_function_sigs(N, A, Encoded) | More].

build_con_facts_nil @
    build_con_facts([], Out) <=> Out = [].

build_con_facts_cons @
    build_con_facts([Td|Tds], Out) <=>
    build_td_con_facts(Td, type_cons(Td), Here),
    build_con_facts(Tds, More),
    Out is append(Here, More).

build_td_con_facts_nil @
    build_td_con_facts(_, [], Out) <=> Out = [].

% Parent type and field types share the declaration's type variables, so
% `copy_term`ing the signature at a use site keeps the parent/field
% sharing that makes `list(T)` relate its element type to `[X|Xs]`.
build_td_con_facts_cons @
    build_td_con_facts(type_def(TN, Vars, Kind, Loc), [data_con(CN, Fields)|Dcs], Out) <=>
    alloc_flex_tvars(sort_unique_tvars(Vars), TVars),
    build_td_con_facts(type_def(TN, Vars, Kind, Loc), Dcs, More),
    Slots is tvar_slots(Vars, TVars),
    FieldTys is encode_tys(TVars, Fields),
    Out = [df_con_sig(CN, csig(tcon(TN, Slots), FieldTys)) | More].

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Rules
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------

:- chr_constraint
    walk_program(tc_env, program, list(decl_fact)),
    walk_rules_from(tc_env, program, list(decl_fact), list(rule)),
    walk_rules(tc_env, list(rule), int),
    walk_rule(tc_env, rule, int).

% Rules are numbered from zero, and `pure.chr` numbers the arity
% errors it reports the same way. The two numberings have to agree: a
% rule's arity error and its solver errors must land in one unit, or
% warning suppression drops the wrong warnings.
%
% Rules first, then the functions, off one `decl_rigid_supply`: a rigid
% type variable's synthetic id is user-visible as `T#n`, so the
% allocation order is part of what a diagnostic says, and one counter
% across both keeps it stated in one place.
walk_program1 @
    walk_program(Env, P, Facts) <=>
    decl_rigid_supply(0),
    walk_rules_from(Env, P, Facts, program_rules(P)).

% The rule list is handed on rather than recomputed: there is no `let`,
% so a value two goals need becomes a helper's parameter.
walk_rules_from1 @
    walk_rules_from(Env, P, Facts, Rules) <=>
    walk_rules(Env, Rules, 0),
    walk_funs(Env, Facts, program_funs(P), length(Rules)).

:- function program_rules(program) -> list(rule).
program_rules(program(Rules, _, _, _, _)) -> Rules.

:- function program_funs(program) -> list(fun_def).
program_funs(program(_, Funs, _, _, _)) -> Funs.

walk_rules_nil @
    walk_rules(_, [], _) <=> true.

walk_rules_cons @
    walk_rules(Env, [R|Rs], Unit) <=>
    walk_rule(Env, R, Unit),
    walk_rules(Env, Rs, Unit + 1).

% A rule is one checking unit: head, guards and body share a unit id
% even though each section is attributed to its own source annotation.
% HNF-synthetic guards are attributed to the *head*, where the patterns
% they were derived from are written — a rule with no user guard has no
% guard text at all.
walk_rule1 @
    walk_rule(Env,
              rule(Name,
                   ann(HAnn, HLoc, rule_head(Kept, Removed)),
                   ann(GAnn, GLoc, Guards),
                   ann(BAnn, BLoc, Body)),
              Unit) <=>
    HeadCons is append(Kept, Removed),
    Label is rule_label(Name),
    HeadCtx = ctx(unit(Unit), origin_ann(HAnn), Label, HLoc),
    alloc_var_types(collect_vars_rule(HeadCons, Guards, Body),
                    head_var_skeletons(Env, HeadCons, Guards),
                    VarTypes),
    head_cons_check(scope(Env, VarTypes, [], HeadCtx), HeadCons, [], Amb),
    check_guards(scope(Env, VarTypes, Amb,
                       ctx(unit(Unit), origin_ann(GAnn), Label, GLoc)),
                 scope(Env, VarTypes, Amb, HeadCtx),
                 Guards, []),
    check_body_goals(scope(Env, VarTypes, Amb,
                           ctx(unit(Unit), origin_ann(BAnn), Label, BLoc)),
                     Body).

% --- Head occurrences -----------------------------------------------

:- chr_constraint
    head_cons_check(scope, list(head_con),
                    list(pair(amb_key, list(fun_sig_t))),
                    list(pair(amb_key, list(fun_sig_t)))),
    head_con_check(scope, head_con,
                   list(pair(amb_key, list(fun_sig_t))),
                   list(pair(amb_key, list(fun_sig_t)))),
    type_of_head_args(scope, list(head_arg), list(ty)),
    unify_zip(list(ty), list(ty), ctx).

head_cons_check_nil @
    head_cons_check(_, [], Amb, Out) <=> Out = Amb.

head_cons_check_cons @
    head_cons_check(S, [C|Cs], Amb, Out) <=>
    head_con_check(S, C, Amb, Amb2),
    head_cons_check(S, Cs, Amb2, Out).

% A rule-head occurrence is an *implementation site*, not a use site: it
% allocates the constraint's declared type parameters as fresh **rigid**
% identities, per occurrence, and types the head's variables through the
% declared argument types. Rigidity is what makes the body correct for
% an arbitrary instantiation — the store is a heterogeneous multiset, so
% two occurrences of one polymorphic constraint may match independently
% chosen instances. Where matching does force two occurrences to agree,
% HNF turns the sharing into a `guard_equal`, whose evidence merges the
% two skolems.
head_con_check1 @
    head_con_check(S, head_con(N, Args), AmbIn, Out) <=>
    DeclTys is env_constraint_types(scope_env(S), con_key(N, length(Args))),
    Bounds is env_constraint_bounds(scope_env(S), con_key(N, length(Args))),
    alloc_rigid_tvars(sort_unique_tvars(append(collect_ty_vars(DeclTys),
                                               bound_sigs_vars(Bounds))),
                      TVars),
    type_of_head_args(S, Args, ArgTys),
    unify_zip(ArgTys, encode_tys(TVars, DeclTys), scope_ctx(S)),
    Out is add_ambients(AmbIn, Bounds, TVars).

type_of_head_args_nil @
    type_of_head_args(_, [], Out) <=> Out = [].

type_of_head_args_var @
    type_of_head_args(S, [head_var(V)|As], Out) <=>
    type_of_head_args(S, As, Rest),
    Out is [scope_var_ty(S, V) | Rest].

type_of_head_args_wild @
    type_of_head_args(S, [head_wild|As], Out) <=>
    type_of_head_args(S, As, Rest),
    Out = [_Slot | Rest].

% Source-variable type first, declared type second — the argument order
% the `tc_unify` rules read. Stops at the shorter list, as `zipWithM_`
% does.
unify_zip_nil @
    unify_zip([], _, _) <=> true.

unify_zip_short @
    unify_zip(_, [], _) <=> true.

unify_zip_cons @
    unify_zip([A|As], [D|Ds], Ctx) <=>
    check_unify(A, D, Ctx),
    unify_zip(As, Ds, Ctx).

% A rule-head occurrence of a bounded constraint *assumes* its bound:
% the required signatures enter the scope's ambient table, which every
% use site in the rule then sees. Their type variables are the
% occurrence's own rigids, which is what keeps a call that resolves
% through an ambient signature polymorphic.
%
% Entries for one `(name, arity)` accumulate; the order among them is
% not observable — resolution requires every surviving candidate to be
% equal after substitution, and bound discharge is an existence check.
:- function add_ambients(list(pair(amb_key, list(fun_sig_t))),
                         list(bound_sig), list(pair(string, ty)))
    -> list(pair(amb_key, list(fun_sig_t))).
add_ambients(Acc, Bounds, TVars) ->
    foldl(fun(Table, Bound) -> add_ambient(Table, Bound, TVars) end, Acc, Bounds).

:- function add_ambient(list(pair(amb_key, list(fun_sig_t))),
                        bound_sig, list(pair(string, ty)))
    -> list(pair(amb_key, list(fun_sig_t))).
add_ambient(Acc, bound_sig(N, _, Args, Ret, _), TVars) ->
    assoc_put_with(fun(New, Old) -> append(Old, New) end,
                   amb_key(N, length(Args)),
                   [fsig(encode_tys(TVars, Args), encode_ty(TVars, Ret))],
                   Acc).

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Guards
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% The list is processed
% left-to-right, threading the constructor each `guard_match`
% established into the `guard_getarg`s that read the same term. Keying
% by operand rather than remembering the most recent match is
% load-bearing: HNF decomposes a compound depth-first, so a nested
% pattern's match sits *between* its parent's extractions.
%
% The two scopes differ only in what their diagnostics point at, and are
% named for it: `UserS` attributes to the user's guard text, which is
% where a `guard_expr` is written, and `SynthS` to the head pattern,
% which is where everything HNF synthesized came from.

:- chr_constraint
    check_guards(scope, scope, list(guard), list(pair(any, match_state))),
    check_guard(scope, scope, guard,
                list(pair(any, match_state)), list(pair(any, match_state))),
    check_match(scope, expr, any, int,
                list(pair(any, match_state)), list(pair(any, match_state))),
    getarg_scrutinee(scope, any, expr, int, maybe(match_state)),
    getarg_slot(scope, any, expr, int, maybe(match_state), maybe(ty)),
    getarg_declared(scope, any, expr, int, any),
    tell_predicate_evidence(scope, maybe(pair(any, ty))).

check_guards_nil @
    check_guards(_, _, [], _) <=> true.

check_guards_cons @
    check_guards(UserS, SynthS, [G|Gs], M) <=>
    check_guard(UserS, SynthS, G, M, M2),
    check_guards(UserS, SynthS, Gs, M2).

% An evidence form: ask-equality succeeds only on structurally identical
% terms, so the two positions agree on their outermost type constructor
% whenever the guard passes. `eq_unify` carries the second reading too —
% the two HNF halves of a repeated source variable are one variable, so
% a still-flexible half binds rather than staying inert. The operands
% are structural positions and are typed as such; the relation is
% symmetric, so the source order is kept.
check_guard_equal @
    check_guard(_, SynthS, guard_equal(E1, E2), M, MOut) <=>
    type_of_expr_struct(SynthS, E1, T1),
    type_of_expr_struct(SynthS, E2, T2),
    eq_unify(T1, T2, ev_top, scope_ctx(SynthS)),
    MOut = M.

check_guard_match_g @
    check_guard(_, SynthS, guard_match(Operand, Con, Arity), M, MOut) <=>
    check_match(SynthS, Operand, canonicalize_con(scope_cons(SynthS), Con),
                Arity, M, MOut).

check_guard_getarg_g @
    check_guard(_, SynthS, guard_getarg(V, Operand, Idx), M, MOut) <=>
    getarg_scrutinee(SynthS, V, Operand, Idx,
                     match_state_for(M, guard_operand_var(Operand))),
    MOut = M.

% The ordinary call check still runs; a type-predicate guard's evidence
% is extra information, not a replacement.
check_guard_expr_g @
    check_guard(UserS, _, guard_expr(E), M, MOut) <=>
    type_of_expr(UserS, E, T),
    check_guard_bool(T, scope_ctx(UserS)),
    tell_predicate_evidence(UserS, type_predicate_fact(E)),
    MOut = M.

% One fresh slot per field of the matched constructor, and one fresh
% *rigid* per parameter of its parent type. The rigids are used only at
% a rigid scrutinee, where the match pins the skolem to the parent type
% at these parameters: the matched value's field types are store-chosen,
% so nothing downstream may assume more about them than the match
% proved.
check_match_known @
    check_match(SynthS, Operand, Con, Arity, M, MOut) <=>
    known_con_with_arity(scope_cons(SynthS), Con, Arity) |
    type_of_expr(SynthS, Operand, OperandTy),
    fresh_slots(Arity, FieldSlots),
    make_decl_rigids(parent_type_arity(scope_cons(SynthS), Con), Betas),
    check_guard_match(OperandTy, Con, Betas, FieldSlots, scope_ctx(SynthS)),
    MOut is record_match(guard_operand_var(Operand), match_state(Con, FieldSlots), M).

% An undeclared constructor, or one at the wrong arity: nothing is
% emitted, and the empty slot list sends the following extractions down
% the declaration path.
check_match_unknown @
    check_match(_, Operand, Con, _, M, MOut) <=>
    MOut is record_match(guard_operand_var(Operand), match_state(Con, []), M).

:- function record_match(maybe(any), match_state, list(pair(any, match_state)))
    -> list(pair(any, match_state)).
record_match(nothing, _, M) -> M.
record_match(just(V), MS, M) -> assoc_put(V, MS, M).

:- function match_state_for(list(pair(any, match_state)), maybe(any))
    -> maybe(match_state).
match_state_for(_, nothing) -> nothing.
match_state_for(M, just(V)) -> assoc_get(V, M).

% The scrutinee's match state is looked up once and handed on with the
% field slot read off it; there is no `let`, so the hand-off is the
% intermediate constraint.
getarg_scrutinee1 @
    getarg_scrutinee(SynthS, V, Operand, Idx, Scrut) <=>
    getarg_slot(SynthS, V, Operand, Idx, Scrut, field_slot_at(Idx, Scrut)).

% The match on this same term already resolved the field's type at the
% instantiation it determined, so unifying against its slot avoids
% consulting the scrutinee's type a second time — which is what breaks
% extraction at a rigid scrutinee.
getarg_slot_matched @
    getarg_slot(SynthS, V, _, _, _, just(Slot)) <=>
    check_unify(scope_var_ty(SynthS, V), Slot, scope_ctx(SynthS)).

getarg_slot_unmatched @
    getarg_slot(SynthS, V, Operand, Idx, Scrut, nothing) <=>
    getarg_declared(SynthS, V, Operand, Idx, scrutinee_con(V, Scrut)).

% No usable match on this term: resolve the field type from the
% constructor declaration instead. An index past the declared arity is
% left alone — the arity walk reports that site already.
getarg_declared_within @
    getarg_declared(SynthS, V, Operand, Idx, Con) <=>
    within_con_arity(scope_cons(SynthS), Con, Idx) |
    type_of_expr(SynthS, Operand, OperandTy),
    check_guard_getarg(scope_var_ty(SynthS, V), OperandTy, Con, Idx, scope_ctx(SynthS)).

getarg_declared_beyond @
    getarg_declared(_, _, _, _, _) <=> true.

:- function scrutinee_con(any, maybe(match_state)) -> any.
scrutinee_con(VarName, nothing) -> VarName.
scrutinee_con(_, just(match_state(Con, _))) -> Con.

:- function field_slot_at(int, maybe(match_state)) -> maybe(ty).
field_slot_at(_, nothing) -> nothing.
field_slot_at(Idx, just(match_state(_, Slots))) -> nth_maybe(Idx, Slots).

% An unknown constructor is within any arity: nothing is known to
% contradict, so the extraction goes through as a no-op consistency
% check.
:- function within_con_arity(con_env, any, int) -> bool.
within_con_arity(Env, Con, Idx) -> idx_within(Idx, lookup_con(Env, Con)).

:- function idx_within(int, maybe(con_info)) -> bool.
idx_within(_, nothing) -> true.
idx_within(Idx, just(Info)) -> Idx < con_arity(Info).

% The typing fact a type-predicate guard contributes. Only a call of one
% of the prelude type predicates on a *bare variable* qualifies: the
% fact is about that variable's slot and the guard's success entails it
% exactly. `atom/1` is deliberately absent — a nullary constructor
% inhabits many types — as are `var`/`nonvar`/`ground`, whose success
% entails boundness rather than a type.
%
% This list is **provisional**: it names prelude functions directly,
% pending the declaration mechanism by which a function declares itself
% a refinement predicate. Nothing else in the checker keys off function
% names.
:- function type_predicate_fact(expr) -> maybe(pair(any, ty)).
type_predicate_fact(call_e(N, [var_e(V)])) | N == quote(prelude:integer) ->
    just(kv(V, ty_int)).
type_predicate_fact(call_e(N, [var_e(V)])) | N == quote(prelude:float) ->
    just(kv(V, ty_float)).
type_predicate_fact(call_e(N, [var_e(V)])) | N == quote(prelude:string) ->
    just(kv(V, ty_string)).
type_predicate_fact(call_e(N, [var_e(V)])) | N == quote(prelude:boolean) ->
    just(kv(V, tcon(quote(prelude:bool), []))).
type_predicate_fact(_) -> nothing.

tell_predicate_evidence_none @
    tell_predicate_evidence(_, nothing) <=> true.

% The fact the guard establishes goes first and the type the position
% already has second — the order the inaccessible-branch message reads
% them in. Both are whole types, so the depth starts at `ev_top`.
tell_predicate_evidence_some @
    tell_predicate_evidence(S, just(kv(V, Fact))) <=>
    ev_unify(Fact, scope_var_ty(S, V), ev_top, scope_ctx(S)).

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Body goals
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% A rule body's variable slots are fixed for the
% whole body: nothing a goal does replaces them, because nothing narrows
% `any` and no other state flows left to right.

:- chr_constraint
    check_body_goals(scope, list(body_goal)),
    check_body_goal(scope, body_goal),
    emit_constraint_use(scope, any, list(ty)).

check_body_goals_nil @
    check_body_goals(_, []) <=> true.

check_body_goals_cons @
    check_body_goals(S, [G|Gs]) <=>
    check_body_goal(S, G),
    check_body_goals(S, Gs).

check_body_true @
    check_body_goal(_, body_true) <=> true.

% Tell-side constraint arguments are evaluated positions, like every
% other expression position.
check_body_tell @
    check_body_goal(S, body_tell(N, Args)) <=>
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, ArgTys),
    emit_constraint_use(S, N, ArgTys).

% `=` is pure structural unification: neither operand is evaluated at
% run time, so neither is typed by evaluation here.
check_body_unify @
    check_body_goal(S, body_unify(E1, E2)) <=>
    type_of_expr_struct(S, E1, T1),
    type_of_expr_struct(S, E2, T2),
    check_unify(T1, T2, scope_ctx(S)).

% The RHS is an evaluated position, and its result meets the left-hand
% variable's slot through the ordinary meet table. `is` has no special
% narrowing power over `any`.
check_body_is @
    check_body_goal(S, body_is(V, E)) <=>
    type_of_expr(S, E, T),
    check_unify(scope_var_ty(S, V), T, scope_ctx(S)).

check_body_call @
    check_body_goal(S, body_call(N, Args)) <=>
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, ArgTys),
    emit_function_call(S, N, ArgTys, _Ret).

check_body_apply @
    check_body_goal(S, body_apply(F, Args)) <=>
    type_of_expr(S, F, _),
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, _).

check_body_host @
    check_body_goal(S, body_host(_, Args)) <=>
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, _).

emit_constraint_use1 @
    emit_constraint_use(S, N, ArgTys) <=>
    build_bound_cands(S,
                      env_constraint_bounds(scope_env(S), con_key(N, length(ArgTys))),
                      Cands),
    check_constraint_use(N, length(ArgTys), Cands, ArgTys, scope_ctx(S)).

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Expression typing
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% Each `expr`
% constructor maps to one solver query; the call-versus-constructor
% split was decided by the resolver, so nothing here re-derives it.
%
% An `Out` left unbound *is* the answer wherever the type is not known
% before the solver runs: a wildcard's type, and the result slot of a
% call or a constructor application, which the solver then binds.

:- chr_constraint
    type_of_expr(scope, expr, ty),
    type_of_exprs(scope, list(expr), list(ty)),
    type_of_expr_struct(scope, expr, ty),
    type_of_exprs_struct(scope, list(expr), list(ty)),
    type_of_exprs_at(scope, arg_position, list(expr), list(ty)),
    type_of_ctor(scope, arg_position, any, list(expr), ty),
    type_of_ctor_known(scope, arg_position, any, list(expr), ty),
    type_lambda_body(scope, list(expr), ty),
    alloc_lambda_params(list(head_arg), list(pair(any, ty)),
                        list(pair(any, ty)), list(ty)),
    emit_function_call(scope, any, list(ty), ty),
    emit_call_form(scope, any, int, list(fun_sig_t), list(pair(any, bcand)),
                   list(ty), ty),
    emit_function_ref(scope, any, list(ty), ty),
    build_bound_cands(scope, list(bound_sig), list(pair(any, bcand))),
    bound_cand_entries(scope, list(bound_sig), list(pair(any, bcand)),
                       list(pair(any, bcand))).

type_of_expr_var @
    type_of_expr(S, var_e(V), Out) <=> Out is scope_var_ty(S, V).

type_of_expr_int @
    type_of_expr(_, int_e(_), Out) <=> Out = ty_int.

type_of_expr_float @
    type_of_expr(_, float_e(_), Out) <=> Out = ty_float.

type_of_expr_text @
    type_of_expr(_, text_e(_), Out) <=> Out = ty_string.

type_of_expr_wild @
    type_of_expr(_, wild_e, _) <=> true.

type_of_expr_ctor @
    type_of_expr(S, ctor_e(N, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_ctor(S, evaluated_position, N, Args, Out).

type_of_expr_call @
    type_of_expr(S, call_e(N, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, ArgTys),
    emit_function_call(S, N, ArgTys, Out).

type_of_expr_apply @
    type_of_expr(S, apply_e(F, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_expr(S, F, _),
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, _),
    Out = ty_any.

type_of_expr_host @
    type_of_expr(S, host_e(_, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, _),
    Out = ty_any.

type_of_expr_funref @
    type_of_expr(S, funref_e(N, Arity), Out) <=>
    fresh_slots(Arity, ArgTys),
    emit_function_ref(S, N, ArgTys, RetTy),
    Out = fun(ArgTys, RetTy).

% Lambda parameters shadow same-named enclosing variables: each name
% gets a fresh flexible slot, so the lambda neither inherits the outer
% variable's type nor leaks its own back out. A repeated parameter name
% shares one slot across its positions. The lambda's value type is the
% type of its trailing expression.
type_of_expr_lambda @
    type_of_expr(S, lambda_e(Params, Body), Out) <=>
    alloc_lambda_params(Params, [], Bindings, ParamTys),
    type_lambda_body(scope_shadow(S, Bindings), Body, BodyTy),
    Out = fun(ParamTys, BodyTy).

type_of_exprs_nil @
    type_of_exprs(_, [], Out) <=> Out = [].

type_of_exprs_cons @
    type_of_exprs(S, [E|Es], Out) <=>
    type_of_expr(S, E, T),
    type_of_exprs(S, Es, Rest),
    Out = [T | Rest].

% Structural positions: the operands of `=`. The runtime treats these
% positions structurally, so the checker must too — a compound whose
% functor names a declared function is a symbolic term typed `any`, not
% a call, so no use check is emitted and no warning drawn. Constructor
% typing still applies, recursing structurally into the fields.
% Function references and lambdas delegate to the evaluated typing:
% under `=` they lower to callable values, not dead data.
type_of_expr_struct_var @
    type_of_expr_struct(S, var_e(V), Out) <=> Out is scope_var_ty(S, V).

type_of_expr_struct_int @
    type_of_expr_struct(_, int_e(_), Out) <=> Out = ty_int.

type_of_expr_struct_float @
    type_of_expr_struct(_, float_e(_), Out) <=> Out = ty_float.

type_of_expr_struct_text @
    type_of_expr_struct(_, text_e(_), Out) <=> Out = ty_string.

type_of_expr_struct_wild @
    type_of_expr_struct(_, wild_e, _) <=> true.

type_of_expr_struct_ctor @
    type_of_expr_struct(S, ctor_e(N, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_ctor(S, structural_position, N, Args, Out).

type_of_expr_struct_call @
    type_of_expr_struct(S, call_e(_, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_exprs_struct(S, Args, _),
    Out = ty_any.

type_of_expr_struct_apply @
    type_of_expr_struct(S, apply_e(F, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_expr_struct(S, F, _),
    type_of_exprs_struct(S, Args, _),
    Out = ty_any.

type_of_expr_struct_host @
    type_of_expr_struct(S, host_e(_, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_exprs_struct(S, Args, _),
    Out = ty_any.

type_of_expr_struct_funref @
    type_of_expr_struct(S, funref_e(N, Arity), Out) <=>
    type_of_expr(S, funref_e(N, Arity), Out).

type_of_expr_struct_lambda @
    type_of_expr_struct(S, lambda_e(Params, Body), Out) <=>
    type_of_expr(S, lambda_e(Params, Body), Out).

type_of_exprs_struct_nil @
    type_of_exprs_struct(_, [], Out) <=> Out = [].

type_of_exprs_struct_cons @
    type_of_exprs_struct(S, [E|Es], Out) <=>
    type_of_expr_struct(S, E, T),
    type_of_exprs_struct(S, Es, Rest),
    Out = [T | Rest].

type_of_exprs_at_evaluated @
    type_of_exprs_at(S, evaluated_position, Es, Out) <=> type_of_exprs(S, Es, Out).

type_of_exprs_at_structural @
    type_of_exprs_at(S, structural_position, Es, Out) <=>
    type_of_exprs_struct(S, Es, Out).

type_of_ctor1 @
    type_of_ctor(S, Pos, N, Args, Out) <=>
    type_of_ctor_known(S, Pos, canonicalize_con(scope_cons(S), N), Args, Out).

% A constructor at its declared arity emits the use check; everything
% else — an unknown name, or a known one at the wrong arity — is typed
% `any`, with the arguments still walked. Gating on the arity is what
% keeps a spurious inconsistency off a site the arity walk already
% reports.
type_of_ctor_declared @
    type_of_ctor_known(S, Pos, Con, Args, Out) <=>
    known_con_with_arity(scope_cons(S), Con, length(Args)) |
    type_of_exprs_at(S, Pos, Args, ArgTys),
    check_constructor_use(Con, ArgTys, Out, scope_ctx(S)).

type_of_ctor_unknown @
    type_of_ctor_known(S, Pos, _, Args, Out) <=>
    type_of_exprs_at(S, Pos, Args, _),
    Out = ty_any.

type_lambda_body_last @
    type_lambda_body(S, [E], Out) <=> type_of_expr(S, E, Out).

type_lambda_body_init @
    type_lambda_body(S, [E|Es], Out) <=>
    type_of_expr(S, E, _),
    type_lambda_body(S, Es, Out).

alloc_lambda_params_nil @
    alloc_lambda_params([], Acc, Bindings, Tys) <=> Bindings = Acc, Tys = [].

alloc_lambda_params_repeat @
    alloc_lambda_params([head_var(V)|Ps], Acc, Bindings, Tys) <=>
    assoc_member(V, Acc) |
    alloc_lambda_params(Ps, Acc, Bindings, Rest),
    Tys is [slot_or_any(assoc_get(V, Acc)) | Rest].

% The unification comes first so that `T` — a slot that exists only
% here — is allocated before the recursive call carries it into the
% accumulator: a variable nested inside a constraint argument is not
% introduced by the call, only a top-level one is.
alloc_lambda_params_var @
    alloc_lambda_params([head_var(V)|Ps], Acc, Bindings, Tys) <=>
    Tys = [T | Rest],
    alloc_lambda_params(Ps, [kv(V, T)|Acc], Bindings, Rest).

alloc_lambda_params_wild @
    alloc_lambda_params([head_wild|Ps], Acc, Bindings, Tys) <=>
    alloc_lambda_params(Ps, Acc, Bindings, Rest),
    Tys = [_Slot | Rest].

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Call and reference emission
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% A call routes through the ambient form when its target name has
% ambient signatures in this scope — i.e. the call sits under a
% bounded constraint's head occurrence — and through the plain form
% otherwise;
% the solver handles both with the same resolution mechanism.

emit_function_call1 @
    emit_function_call(S, N, ArgTys, RetTy) <=>
    build_bound_cands(S,
                      env_function_bounds(scope_env(S), amb_key(N, length(ArgTys))),
                      Cands),
    emit_call_form(S, N, length(ArgTys),
                   scope_ambient(S, amb_key(N, length(ArgTys))),
                   Cands, ArgTys, RetTy).

emit_call_plain @
    emit_call_form(S, N, A, [], Cands, ArgTys, RetTy) <=>
    check_function_use(N, A, Cands, ArgTys, RetTy, scope_ctx(S)).

emit_call_ambient @
    emit_call_form(S, N, A, Ambs, Cands, ArgTys, RetTy) <=>
    check_function_use_with_ambient(N, A, Ambs, Cands, ArgTys, RetTy, scope_ctx(S)).

% A `fun name/arity` reference is checked against the expected function
% type at its use site; for a class referent the return component
% participates in the residual filtering, which is why the ambient list
% travels even when it is empty.
emit_function_ref1 @
    emit_function_ref(S, N, ArgTys, RetTy) <=>
    build_bound_cands(S,
                      env_function_bounds(scope_env(S), amb_key(N, length(ArgTys))),
                      Cands),
    check_function_ref(N, length(ArgTys),
                       scope_ambient(S, amb_key(N, length(ArgTys))),
                       Cands, ArgTys, RetTy, scope_ctx(S)).

% One `bc(ambients, declared)` per bound-named function, keyed on that
% name and with same-named bounds merged. The discharge rules are pure
% and cannot gather candidates across the store, so the whole candidate
% set is assembled here and travels by value. Ambient and declared
% candidates are kept apart because they discharge differently.
build_bound_cands1 @
    build_bound_cands(S, Bounds, Out) <=> bound_cand_entries(S, Bounds, [], Out).

bound_cand_entries_nil @
    bound_cand_entries(_, [], Acc, Out) <=> Out = Acc.

bound_cand_entries_cons @
    bound_cand_entries(S, [bound_sig(N, _, Args, _, _)|Bs], Acc, Out) <=>
    encode_sigs(env_function_sigs(scope_env(S), N), Decls),
    bound_cand_entries(S, Bs,
                       assoc_put_with(fun merge_bc/2, N,
                                      bc(scope_ambient(S,
                                                       amb_key(N, length(Args))),
                                         Decls),
                                      Acc),
                       Out).

% The combining function `assoc_put_with` calls with the newcomer
% first: an existing entry keeps its position in the list and its
% candidates keep precedence over the ones being merged in, which is
% the order the discharge rules search.
:- function merge_bc(bcand, bcand) -> bcand.
merge_bc(New, Old) -> bc(append(bc_ambs(Old), bc_ambs(New)),
                         append(bc_decls(Old), bc_decls(New))).

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Functions
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% Checking a plain function's equations.
% Each equation is its own checking unit, including one contributed by
% an `:- extend_function` / `:- extend_class` directive: extension
% equations share the owning declaration's annotation and so its source
% location, and only the unit id keeps their diagnostics apart.
%
% Unit ids continue from the rules, one per equation of a *plain*
% function; a class function's equations get none, because the old
% checker checks them in throwaway sessions and collects what survives
% outside any unit. `pure.chr`'s `funs_arity_errors` numbers the
% same way, and must keep doing so: an equation's arity error and its
% solver errors have to share a unit or suppression drops the wrong
% warnings.

:- chr_constraint
    walk_funs(tc_env, list(decl_fact), list(fun_def), int),
    walk_fun(tc_env, fun_def, int),
    walk_fun_eqs(tc_env, any, int, loc, fun_sig, list(bound_sig),
                 list(fun_eq), int),
    check_equation(tc_env, any, int, loc, fun_sig, list(bound_sig), fun_eq, int),
    check_equation_at(scope, list(pair(string, ty)), fun_sig, list(bound_sig),
                      fun_eq),
    tell_equation_bounds(scope, list(pair(string, ty)), list(bound_sig)),
    check_fun_stmts(scope, list(fun_stmt), scope),
    check_fun_stmt(scope, fun_stmt, scope),
    fun_is_bind(scope, any, ty, scope).

walk_funs_nil @
    walk_funs(_, _, [], _) <=> true.

% Two or more declared signatures is a class function: its equations are
% checked per candidate signature in isolated sub-sessions below, and
% consume no unit id here. `pure.chr`'s `fun_unit_count` makes the
% same split.
walk_funs_class @
    walk_funs(Env, Facts, [F|Fs], Unit) <=> fun_sig_count(F) > 1 |
    walk_class_fun(Env, Facts, F),
    walk_funs(Env, Facts, Fs, Unit).

walk_funs_plain @
    walk_funs(Env, Facts, [F|Fs], Unit) <=>
    walk_fun(Env, F, Unit),
    walk_funs(Env, Facts, Fs, Unit + fun_eq_count(F)).

:- function fun_sig_count(fun_def) -> int.
fun_sig_count(fun_def(_, _, Sigs, _, _)) -> length(Sigs).

:- function fun_eq_count(fun_def) -> int.
fun_eq_count(fun_def(_, _, _, _, ann(_, _, Eqs))) -> length(Eqs).

% The effective single signature. An untyped function checks exactly
% like its all-`any` spelled-out form, which mentions no type variables
% and so allocates no rigids; its bounds are dropped, since a
% user-written untyped function cannot carry `requiring`, and a lifted
% lambda's inherited clause is ignored here exactly as
% `tell_function_decls_untyped` ignores it.
walk_fun_untyped @
    walk_fun(Env, fun_def(N, A, [], _, ann(EAnn, ELoc, Eqs)), Unit) <=>
    walk_fun_eqs(Env, N, EAnn, ELoc, all_any_sig(A), [], Eqs, Unit).

walk_fun_typed @
    walk_fun(Env, fun_def(N, _, [Sig], Bounds, ann(EAnn, ELoc, Eqs)), Unit) <=>
    walk_fun_eqs(Env, N, EAnn, ELoc, Sig, Bounds, Eqs, Unit).

walk_fun_eqs_nil @
    walk_fun_eqs(_, _, _, _, _, _, [], _) <=> true.

walk_fun_eqs_cons @
    walk_fun_eqs(Env, N, EAnn, ELoc, Sig, Bounds, [Eq|Eqs], Unit) <=>
    check_equation(Env, N, EAnn, ELoc, Sig, Bounds, Eq, Unit),
    walk_fun_eqs(Env, N, EAnn, ELoc, Sig, Bounds, Eqs, Unit + 1).

% An equation of a single-signature function (untyped, bounded or
% unbounded). The declared type variables become *rigid* identities
% shared between the parameter types, the result type and any ambient
% signatures a `requiring` clause contributes: that sharing is what
% keeps a call resolved through an ambient signature polymorphic, and
% what makes an unbounded polymorphic body calling an overloaded
% operator at its own type parameter fail rather than silently pass.
% One equation against the function's single declared signature.
check_equation1 @
    check_equation(Env, FName, EAnn, ELoc, fun_sig(ArgTys, RetTy), Bounds, Eq, Unit) <=>
    alloc_var_types(collect_vars_eq(Eq),
                    param_var_skeletons(Env, eq_params(Eq), ArgTys, eq_guards(Eq)),
                    VarTypes),
    alloc_rigid_tvars(sort_unique_tvars(append(append(collect_ty_vars(ArgTys),
                                                      collect_ty_vars_expr(RetTy)),
                                               bound_sigs_vars(Bounds))),
                      TVars),
    check_equation_at(scope(Env, VarTypes, add_ambients([], Bounds, TVars),
                            ctx(unit(Unit), origin_ann(EAnn),
                                label_function(FName), ELoc)),
                      TVars, fun_sig(ArgTys, RetTy), Bounds, Eq).

:- function eq_params(fun_eq) -> list(head_arg).
eq_params(fun_eq(Params, _, _, _)) -> Params.

:- function eq_guards(fun_eq) -> list(guard).
eq_guards(fun_eq(_, Guards, _, _)) -> Guards.

% Bounds, then parameters, then guards, then the prelude statements,
% then the result. The order is the operational one — an equation's
% guards run after pattern matching and before the right-hand side —
% and it is load-bearing: a guard's typing fact must already be told
% when the prelude's and the result's constraints are gathered.
%
% Parameters and guards share the equation's annotation, so the same
% context attributes both; `check_guards` is handed it twice for that
% reason.
check_equation_at1 @
    check_equation_at(S, TVars, fun_sig(ArgTys, RetTy), Bounds,
                      fun_eq(Params, Guards, Prelude, Rhs)) <=>
    tell_equation_bounds(S, TVars, Bounds),
    type_of_head_args(S, Params, ParamTys),
    unify_zip(ParamTys, encode_tys(TVars, ArgTys), scope_ctx(S)),
    check_guards(S, S, Guards, []),
    check_fun_stmts(S, Prelude, S2),
    type_of_expr(S2, Rhs, RhsTy),
    check_unify(RhsTy, encode_ty(TVars, RetTy), scope_ctx(S)).

tell_equation_bounds_nil @
    tell_equation_bounds(_, _, []) <=> true.

% The equation site's own bounds.
%
% Each of these discharges trivially and unconditionally: the ambient
% candidate list is the very list `add_ambients` built from these same
% bounds at these same rigids, so `discharge_bound_ok_amb`'s existential
% test always finds an exactly-equal witness and `discharge_bound_fail`
% can never rule every candidate out. The check therefore cannot report
% anything, and no golden covers it — deleting it leaves the whole
% corpus green. It is kept because the emission is uniform — every
% equation of a bounded function tells its own function's bounds — and
% because the discharge rules are the solver's and may grow: a bound
% that stops being self-satisfying should start failing here.
tell_equation_bounds_cons @
    tell_equation_bounds(S, TVars, [bound_sig(N, _, Args, Ret, _)|Bs]) <=>
    encode_sigs(env_function_sigs(scope_env(S), N), Decls),
    check_bound(N, scope_ambient(S, amb_key(N, length(Args))), Decls,
                encode_tys(TVars, Args), encode_ty(TVars, Ret), scope_ctx(S)),
    tell_equation_bounds(S, TVars, Bs).

% --- Prelude statements ---------------------------------------------
%
% Only `fun_is` changes the scope: it rebinds its variable to a
% *fresh* slot, mirroring the
% runtime's lexical shadowing (`compileFunStmt` emits a `LetVal`, not a
% unification), so later statements and the result expression see the
% new slot while the right-hand side is still typed against the old one.

check_fun_stmts_nil @
    check_fun_stmts(S, [], Out) <=> Out = S.

check_fun_stmts_cons @
    check_fun_stmts(S, [St|Sts], Out) <=>
    check_fun_stmt(S, St, S2),
    check_fun_stmts(S2, Sts, Out).

% The right-hand side is typed first, against the scope the statement
% starts in, and only then is the shadowing slot decided — which is the
% order that makes `N is N + 1` check against the outer `N`.
check_fun_stmt_is @
    check_fun_stmt(S, fun_is(V, E), Out) <=>
    type_of_expr(S, E, T),
    fun_is_bind(S, V, T, Out).

% Rebinding a declared-`any` local keeps `any`, whatever the right-hand
% side evaluates to. Nothing narrows `any`, and that has to include the
% shadowing slot: were the rebound local to take the right-hand side's
% type, `is` would be a back-door refinement of exactly the variables
% the declaration said to leave alone.
fun_is_bind_any @
    fun_is_bind(S, V, _, Out) <=> scope_var_ty(S, V) == ty_any |
    Out is scope_shadow(S, [kv(V, ty_any)]).

% The unification comes first so that the fresh slot is allocated: only
% a top-level argument of a tell introduces a variable.
fun_is_bind_fresh @
    fun_is_bind(S, V, T, Out) <=>
    check_unify(NewSlot, T, scope_ctx(S)),
    Out is scope_shadow(S, [kv(V, NewSlot)]).

check_fun_stmt_host @
    check_fun_stmt(S, fun_host(_, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, _),
    Out = S.

check_fun_stmt_call @
    check_fun_stmt(S, fun_call(N, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, ArgTys),
    emit_function_call(S, N, ArgTys, _Ret),
    Out = S.

check_fun_stmt_apply @
    check_fun_stmt(S, fun_apply(F, Args), Out) <=>
    type_of_expr(S, F, _),
    type_of_exprs(S, Args, _),
    Out = S.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% The class-function overload search
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% Each equation of a multi-signature class function must check under
% at least one declared signature, with its
% parameters typed by that signature's argument types and its result
% checked against that signature's return type. Signatures are tried in
% declaration order and the search stops at the first that fits; an
% equation that fits none reports one `no_matching_overload`.
%
% Every attempt runs in its own session: CHR has no backtracking, and
% the program-wide session has a single error accumulator. An attempt's
% diagnostics describe the failed fit rather than the program and are
% discarded — including its warnings, since under a candidate signature
% a contradicting evidence fact counts as failure of that candidate and
% emits nothing. Suppression runs inside the attempt, so a surviving
% warning means the equation contradicted the signature without
% erroring.

% Whether one (equation x signature) attempt fitted. An attempt that
% raised a hard error leaves the variable *unbound* — `run_chr_session`
% answers `false` and binds nothing — which the retry rule reads as a
% failure, so there is no third verdict. Worth knowing when reading a
% surprising report: an error thrown inside an attempt is a bug in the
% checker, and it surfaces as a spurious `no_matching_overload` rather
% than as itself.
:- chr_type attempt_verdict ---> attempt_ok ; attempt_failed.

:- chr_constraint
    walk_class_fun(tc_env, list(decl_fact), fun_def),
    class_eqs(tc_env, list(decl_fact), any, int, loc, list(fun_sig), list(fun_eq)),
    class_eq_try(tc_env, list(decl_fact), any, int, loc, fun_eq, list(fun_sig)),
    class_attempt(tc_env, list(decl_fact), any, int, loc, fun_eq, fun_sig,
                  attempt_verdict),
    class_eq_next(tc_env, list(decl_fact), any, int, loc, fun_eq, list(fun_sig),
                  attempt_verdict),
    check_attempt(tc_env, list(decl_fact), any, int, loc, fun_sig, fun_eq,
                  attempt_verdict).

walk_class_fun1 @
    walk_class_fun(Env, Facts, fun_def(N, _, Sigs, _, ann(EAnn, ELoc, Eqs))) <=>
    class_eqs(Env, Facts, N, EAnn, ELoc, Sigs, Eqs).

class_eqs_nil @
    class_eqs(_, _, _, _, _, _, []) <=> true.

class_eqs_cons @
    class_eqs(Env, Facts, N, EAnn, ELoc, Sigs, [Eq|Eqs]) <=>
    class_eq_try(Env, Facts, N, EAnn, ELoc, Eq, Sigs),
    class_eqs(Env, Facts, N, EAnn, ELoc, Sigs, Eqs).

% No signature left: the equation fits none of them. The diagnostic is
% attributed to no unit — it belongs to the function as a whole rather
% than to one of its equations' checking units — so it suppresses
% nothing.
%
% The name travels wrapped in `source_name`, which asks the decoder for
% the real inverse of the mangling; the `no_matching_overload`s the
% solver reports travel as `mangled_name` and render with it only half
% undone. The two spellings differ only for a name the mangling
% escaped, and both are user-visible, so they are kept apart rather
% than unified. See `decodeError` in
% `src/YCHR/Internal/TypeCheck.hs`.
class_eq_try_none @
    class_eq_try(_, _, FName, EAnn, ELoc, _, []) <=>
    report_error(ctx(no_unit, origin_ann(EAnn), label_function(FName), ELoc),
                 no_matching_overload, source_name(FName)).

class_eq_try_sig @
    class_eq_try(Env, Facts, FName, EAnn, ELoc, Eq, [Sig|Sigs]) <=>
    class_attempt(Env, Facts, FName, EAnn, ELoc, Eq, Sig, Verdict),
    class_eq_next(Env, Facts, FName, EAnn, ELoc, Eq, Sigs, Verdict).

% `Verdict` reaches the sub-session as a shared logical variable, which
% is how the answer comes back; everything else in the goal is ground
% AST, so nothing carrying a live rigid pin cell crosses the boundary.
% The variable has to be introduced by the *caller* — a variable nested
% inside `quote/1` on the right of an `is` is not — hence this separate
% constraint, whose head argument it is.
class_attempt1 @
    class_attempt(Env, Facts, FName, EAnn, ELoc, Eq, Sig, Verdict) <=>
    _Ran is run_chr_session(quote(check_attempt(Env, Facts, FName, EAnn, ELoc,
                                                Sig, Eq, Verdict))).

class_eq_next_ok @
    class_eq_next(_, _, _, _, _, _, _, V) <=> V == attempt_ok | true.

class_eq_next_retry @
    class_eq_next(Env, Facts, FName, EAnn, ELoc, Eq, Sigs, _) <=>
    class_eq_try(Env, Facts, FName, EAnn, ELoc, Eq, Sigs).

% One attempt, in its own session. Both the environment and the
% declaration facts travel by value: the environment is ground AST, and
% the facts are copied on arrival (`retell_decls`), so the attempt
% starts from a complete declaration environment without re-deriving
% one and without sharing a variable with the session it came from.
%
% The unit id is 0 and the diagnostics never leave, so neither the
% number nor the annotation id is observable. Suppression runs because
% an attempt is an ordinary session and drains its accumulators like
% one; it cannot change the verdict, since it only drops warnings when
% there is an error, and an error already fails the attempt.
check_attempt1 @
    check_attempt(Env, Facts, FName, EAnn, ELoc, Sig, Eq, Verdict) <=>
    errors([]),
    warnings([]),
    rigid_supply(-1),
    decl_rigid_supply(0),
    retell_decls(Facts),
    check_equation(Env, FName, EAnn, ELoc, Sig, [], Eq, 0),
    collect(Errs),
    collect_warnings(Warns),
    Verdict is verdict_of(Errs, suppress_warnings(Errs, Warns)).

:- function verdict_of(list(error), list(warning)) -> attempt_verdict.
verdict_of([], []) -> attempt_ok.
verdict_of(_, _) -> attempt_failed.

% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
% Goals
% ---------------------------------------------------------------------
%
% A query's goals are checked exactly like a rule body — same
% `check_body_goal`, same declarations — with two
% differences the shape below spells out: the whole goal list shares one
% variable scope, so a name two goals mention denotes one slot, and each
% goal is nonetheless its own checking unit, so a goal's own error
% suppresses only its own warnings.
%
% That second half is unobservable today: the only warning the checker
% emits needs a guard, and a goal has none, so no goal-level warning
% exists for a unit id to keep or drop. Renumbering every goal to one
% unit leaves the whole corpus green. It is per goal because that is
% what the spec says the unit is (§Type Checking Procedure), and so
% that a goal-level warning added later suppresses correctly without
% anyone having to notice this.
%
% There is no head and there are no guards, so nothing contributes a
% skeleton (every slot starts flexible) and no ambient signature is in
% scope. The caller supplies the location and the label; goals have no
% annotated node to echo, hence `origin_none`.

:- chr_constraint
    walk_goals(tc_env, list(body_goal), loc, diag_label),
    walk_goals_at(tc_env, list(body_goal), loc, diag_label,
                  list(pair(any, ty)), int).

% `decl_rigid_supply` is seeded even though nothing here can consume it:
% rigids come from head occurrences, `guard_match` betas and declared
% type parameters, none of which a goal has. Without the seed a walker
% that grew one of those would not fail — `make_decl_rigid` would simply
% suspend and leave the type unbound.
walk_goals1 @
    walk_goals(Env, Goals, Loc, Label) <=>
    decl_rigid_supply(0),
    alloc_var_types(collect_vars_goals(Goals), [], VarTypes),
    walk_goals_at(Env, Goals, Loc, Label, VarTypes, 0).

walk_goals_at_nil @
    walk_goals_at(_, [], _, _, _, _) <=> true.

walk_goals_at_cons @
    walk_goals_at(Env, [G|Gs], Loc, Label, VarTypes, Unit) <=>
    check_body_goal(scope(Env, VarTypes, [], ctx(unit(Unit), origin_none, Label, Loc)),
                    G),
    walk_goals_at(Env, Gs, Loc, Label, VarTypes, Unit + 1).
